# Argus environment. Copy to .env (gitignored) in the repo, or to
# ~/.config/argus/.env (scripts/install-linux.sh does that for you), and fill in
# the keys you have. Every key is optional: keyless feeds work without any.
#
# GUARDRAIL: never put a secret in a VITE_-prefixed variable. Vite inlines those
# into the browser bundle. Every real key/secret belongs to the proxy (server
# side) only. Client-side env is for non-secret config toggles.

# --- proxy (server side) ---
# PROXY_PORT=8787
# PROXY_HOST=127.0.0.1                 # unset = all interfaces (needed for the phone)
# PROXY_HTTPS=true                     # or run: npm run start:https
# PROXY_TLS_KEY=/path/to/key.pem       # production cert (else self-signed)
# PROXY_TLS_CERT=/path/to/cert.pem
# PROXY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=*              # comma list to pin CORS origins
# CT_STREAM_URL=wss://...              # CertStream-compatible stream for the CT ticker
# PROXY_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
#
# Feed secrets, referenced by name from proxy/feeds.js:
# OpenSky OAuth2 client-credentials (register a client at opensky-network.org).
# OPENSKY_CLIENT_ID=
# OPENSKY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# NASA FIRMS MAP_KEY for the fires layer (free, firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov).
# FIRMS_MAP_KEY=
# AISStream API key for the ships websocket (free, aisstream.io).
# AISSTREAM_API_KEY=
# Google Maps Platform key for Photorealistic 3D Tiles (the terrain "Photoreal"
# toggle). Server-side only; brokered at /tiles/google. Without it, that toggle
# degrades gracefully and stays on 3D terrain.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Shodan (awareness-only density layer + host exposure in `correlate`). The
# layer shows one curated snapshot (VIEW > SHODAN) from the credit-free count
# endpoint, cached 12 h at the proxy; the optional host sample costs one query
# credit per snapshot. InternetDB (open ports and CVEs per IP) needs no key.
# SHODAN_API_KEY=

# TomTom key for the "Traffic flow" and "Traffic incidents" layers (free tier,
# developer.tomtom.com). Injected by the proxy as ?key= (flow raster tiles and
# the Incident Details API); it never reaches the browser. Free allowances:
# about 200,000 tiles a month and about 2,500 non-tile requests a day, so the
# proxy caps flow tiles at 6,000 a day (cached 120 s) and incident requests at
# 2,000 a day (cached 2 min per view box). Without it neither layer is offered.
# The same key gives the simulated traffic its live speeds and the VIEW > ROAD
# FLOW readout (Flow Segment Data, capped at 450 requests a day, cached 2 min
# per point); without it the simulation runs at free-flow speeds.
# TOMTOM_API_KEY=

# Mapillary client token for the "Street photos" layer and the STREET PHOTO
# card action (free, mapillary.com/dashboard/developers; it looks like MLY|...).
# Sent by the proxy as "Authorization: OAuth <token>", never to the browser.
# Photos are CC BY-SA 4.0; every card credits Mapillary and links the image.
# MAPILLARY_TOKEN=
# Thumbnails come through the proxy from Meta's image CDN. If the generic host
# refuses URLs signed for a regional edge, name that edge host here.
# MAPILLARY_IMAGE_URL=https://scontent.xx.fbcdn.net

# Optional extras (every one of these layers works without them):
# Launch Library 2 token: raises the keyless 15 calls/hour (thespacedevs.com).
# LL2_API_TOKEN=
# TfL app key: raises TfL's anonymous limit for the JamCam catalogue (api-portal.tfl.gov.uk).
# TFL_APP_KEY=
# Public webcams layer. Windy Webcams API v3 key (free tier, api.windy.com/keys):
# injected by the proxy as the x-windy-api-key header. Free-tier stills are
# preview size and expire after about 10 minutes; attribution with a link back
# to windy.com is required (every card carries it). Without it the layer shows
# NASA EPIC and the observatory stills only.
# WINDY_WEBCAMS_KEY=
# US National Park Service webcams (free key, nps.gov/subjects/developer): sent
# as the X-Api-Key header; 1,000 requests an hour, about one a session.
# NPS_API_KEY=
# More traffic cameras. WSDOT Traveler Information API access code (free,
# wsdot.wa.gov/traffic/api): injected as ?AccessCode=.
# WSDOT_ACCESS_CODE=
# 511-platform state DOT developer keys (free, each state's 511 /developers
# site): injected as ?key=. Each network appears only once its key is set.
# NY511_KEY=
# AZ511_KEY=
# GA511_KEY=
# WI511_KEY=
# ID511_KEY=
# UDOT_TRAFFIC_KEY=
# AK511_KEY=
# NVROADS_KEY=
# LA511_KEY=
# CTROADS_KEY=

# --- your own equipment and alternative upstreams ---
# Your ADS-B receiver (dump1090 / readsb / tar1090, e.g. an RTL-SDR on this
# machine): the URL of the folder holding aircraft.json. Must be this machine
# or the LAN. Enables the "My receiver" layer.
# LOCAL_ADSB_URL=http://localhost:8080/data
# A 978 MHz UAT decoder (dump978-fa + skyaware978), same document shape:
# LOCAL_UAT_URL=http://localhost:8978/data
# Another Overpass instance (public ones rate-limit hard; one you run is best).
# OVERPASS_URL=https://overpass.example.org/api
# Another copy of the CHP CAD incident file (same /sa.xml path), e.g. the plain
# http host if https is refused: http://media.chp.ca.gov/sa_xml
# CHP_CAD_URL=https://media.chp.ca.gov/sa_xml
# Pin one Radio Browser mirror instead of the round-robin name.
# RADIO_BROWSER_URL=https://de1.api.radio-browser.info

# --- terminal shell ---
# Home position for "Around Me" in `argus tui` (a terminal has no GPS).
# ARGUS_HOME=37.77,-122.42
# The Shodan snapshot the terminal map shows (argus shodan --snapshot list).
# ARGUS_SHODAN_SNAPSHOT=web

# --- client (safe, non-secret only) ---
# Usually unnecessary: the app finds the proxy at its own origin (npm start, or
# the dev server forwarding to localhost:8787). Set this only to call a proxy on
# a different origin. Not a secret.
# VITE_PROXY_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8787
